  "hero.title": "How PostgreSQL Runs a Query",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Software principles · A hands-on long essay",
  "hero.sub": "Send one `SELECT ... WHERE` or `UPDATE` to the database:<br>in what exact order do PostgreSQL's memory buffers and 8 KiB pages actually execute it?",
  "hero.lead": "Writing `UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 42;` followed by `SELECT ... WHERE tier = 'pro' AND balance &gt; 1000` in an application looks like flipping one field and scanning a list. This essay asks one question: <strong>from sending a SQL statement until tuples return and modifications land safely on disk, what happens inside PostgreSQL at every step?</strong> Why does updating a single row never overwrite bytes in place, how do concurrent readers never block writers, why does `AUTOVACUUM` have to keep running or halt the database, and how does the planner choose between a sequential scan, an index scan, and a hash join?",
  "hero.note": "Every byte offset, bit layout, and planner cost constant here comes from the source specifications of <strong>PostgreSQL 17.6</strong> (released 14 August 2025) and <strong>PostgreSQL 18.0</strong> (released 25 September 2025): `bufpage.h`, `htup_details.h`, `procarray.c`, `costsize.c`, `nbtinsert.c`, `freelist.c`, `varsup.c`, `xlog.c`, and `pg_crc32c_sb8.c`. Because no `postgres` or `psql` binary is installed on this host, the page does not ship a live database server: instead, `models.js` reimplements the page-header packing, Castagnoli CRC-32C checksums, MVCC snapshot truth tables, cost equations, clock-sweep buffer pool, B-tree splits, and 32-bit modular XID wraparound in zero-dependency code, and `tools/crosscheck.cjs` verifies them item by item against the source layouts and standard test vectors. This essay was written by an AI; the end of each chapter states what its model leaves out.",
  "ch1.title": "Query pipeline: from SQL text to the Volcano iterator",
  "toc.ch1": "Query pipeline & Volcano model",
  "ch1.law": "PostgreSQL never \"executes SQL text\" directly. Every statement passes through five stages and turns into a pull-based tree of iterators (`ExecProcNode`) yielding one tuple at a time.",
  "ch1.p1": "Think of an order counter behind a kitchen pass. A customer hands over a handwritten slip; the cashier checks the grammar, looks up the menu to verify dish names and prices, expands combo deals, asks the head chef which stove plan is fastest with today's stock, and finally the runner ladles one portion at a time on demand. PostgreSQL executes a query in those exact five steps.",
  "ch1.code.aria": "Two SQL statements followed throughout the essay",
  "ch1.code": "SELECT id, name, balance\nFROM accounts\nWHERE tier = 'pro' AND balance > 1000\nORDER BY balance DESC LIMIT 20;\n\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 42;",
  "ch1.p2": "Once the frontend/backend protocol hands the string to a backend process, the <strong>Parser</strong> runs the lexer and Bison grammar to emit a raw `ParseStmt` tree—without yet knowing whether `accounts` even exists. Next, the <strong>Analyzer</strong> checks system catalogs (`pg_class`, `pg_attribute`) to resolve table OIDs, column types, and permissions into a typed `Query` tree. If any views or rules appear, the <strong>Rewriter</strong> expands them inline.",
  "ch1.p3": "The fourth stage, the <strong>Planner / Optimizer</strong>, picks the strategy: the same `SELECT` might walk an index in order, scan the heap with a top-N heap sort, or hash-aggregate first. It prices candidate physical paths using table statistics and passes the cheapest `Plan` tree to the <strong>Executor</strong>. Execution runs top-down via <strong>`ExecProcNode()`</strong>: the root node pulls one tuple from its child, which pulls one from its child—the classic <strong>Volcano iterator model</strong>, letting a `LIMIT 20` stop early without materializing the rest of the table.",
  "dg.pipeline.cap": "Figure 1. Five stages from SQL string to output rows: the first four compile text into a physical plan tree; the fifth pulls tuples row by row up the iterator chain.",
  "dg.pipeline.aria": "Diagram: Parser, Analyzer, Rewriter, Planner, and Executor five-stage pipeline with intermediate data structures",
