  "ch1.limit": "This chapter draws the map of the pipeline. The next chapter opens the fourth stage—the planner—to see how histograms and I/O weights score competing scans; chapters 3 through 9 follow the executor straight down into 8 KiB pages, MVCC snapshots, shared buffers, and the write-ahead log.",
  "ch2.title": "Cost-based planner & statistics: why indexes sometimes lose",
  "toc.ch2": "Cost-based planner & stats",
  "ch2.law": "The optimizer has no sentimental attachment to indexes; once matching rows scatter across too many pages, hopping between random 8 KiB blocks costs more than reading the whole heap straight through.",
  "ch2.p1": "Suppose a courier has 500 parcels to deliver. If every recipient sits in one office tower, looking up suite numbers wins hands down; if those 500 parcels lie across 900 out of 1,000 streets in town, driving down every block sequentially beats zigzagging back and forth across addresses. PostgreSQL's planner runs that arithmetic on every query.",
  "ch2.p2": "`ANALYZE` records column statistics in `pg_statistic` (exposed via `pg_stats`): Most Common Values (MCVs), equi-depth histograms, and distinct counts `n_distinct`. Functions in `costsize.c` estimate selectivity $p$ and matching row counts, then multiply them by five default cost constants: `seq_page_cost = 1.0`, `random_page_cost = 4.0`, `cpu_tuple_cost = 0.01`, `cpu_index_tuple_cost = 0.005`, and `cpu_operator_cost = 0.0025`.",
  "dg.costpaths.cap": "Figure 2. Access patterns across ten 8 KiB heap pages: Sequential Scan reads straight through, Index Scan jumps randomly in key order, and Bitmap Heap Scan deduplicates block numbers before reading in physical order.",
  "dg.costpaths.aria": "Diagram: comparing block access patterns for Sequential Scan, Index Scan, and Bitmap Heap Scan across ten heap pages",
  "dg.costpaths.seq": "Sequential Scan",
  "dg.costpaths.seqTag": "seq_page_cost=1.0",
  "dg.costpaths.idx": "Index Scan",
  "dg.costpaths.idxTag": "random_page_cost=4.0",
  "dg.costpaths.bmp": "Bitmap Heap Scan",
  "dg.costpaths.bmpTag": "Sort & dedupe page IDs",
  "ch2.p3": "In the model below—a 10,000-page table (40 rows per page, 400,000 rows total) under the default `random_page_cost = 4.0`—at `0.1%` selectivity (400 rows), Index Scan costs just 1,592 against 15,000 for a Sequential Scan. By `5%` selectivity, unclustered Index Scan is swamped by repeated random page fetches in the Mackert–Lohman buffer equation, while <strong>Bitmap Heap Scan</strong> collects and sorts page numbers into a bitmap first for 13,044. Past <strong>10.0%</strong>, Sequential Scan takes the lead outright. Lower `random_page_cost` toward `1.1` for NVMe SSDs and the crossover shifts to 11.8%; turn on all-visible pages in the Visibility Map and <strong>Index-Only Scan</strong> skips heap fetches altogether.",
  "ch2.model.title": "Planner cost crossover: Seq Scan, Index Scan, Bitmap & Index-Only",
  "ch2.model.how": "Drag selectivity to watch the four plan curves cross as matching rows grow; drag random_page_cost between HDD (4.0) and SSD (1.1); tick all-visible pages in the Visibility Map to unlock Index-Only Scan.",
