  "ch2.say.index": "At selectivity {sel} (~{rows} rows) and random_page_cost = {rpc}, <em>Index Scan</em> wins (cost {cost}): so few heap pages are touched that building a bitmap or scanning the whole table cannot pay off.",
  "ch2.say.bitmap": "By selectivity {sel}, <em>Bitmap Heap Scan</em> takes over (cost {cost}): it deduplicates the ~{dp} matching heap pages and reads them in ascending block order instead of thrashing randomly. Seq Scan catches up at {cross}.",
  "ch2.say.seq": "At selectivity {sel} past the crossover, <em>Sequential Scan</em> wins at {cost}: almost every page has to be read anyway, so straight sequential streaming across 10,000 pages beats random index hops.",
  "ch2.say.indexonly": "With every page marked all-visible in the Visibility Map, <em>Index-Only Scan</em> (cost {cost}) never visits the heap at all and answers straight from the B-tree leaves.",
  "ch2.p4": "There is a notorious statistics trap here: the planner assumes single-column predicates are independent by default. If `city = 'Shanghai'` and `country = 'CN'` each match `10%` of rows, multiplying them predicts `1%` (4,000 rows) when in truth they move together and match 38,000 rows—an underestimate of <strong>9.5×</strong>, enough to trick the optimizer into a Nested Loop that runs orders of magnitude slower. Creating multi-column extended statistics (`CREATE STATISTICS`) fixes that independence blind spot.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model omits: multi-table join cardinality, parallel gather worker allocation (`max_parallel_workers_per_gather`), and high physical correlation (`pg_stats.correlation`) discounting random seeks on clustered columns. Cost units are relative `costsize.c` figures, not wall-clock milliseconds.",
  "ch3.title": "Slotted 8 KiB pages & HOT updates: why one update never overwrites in place",
  "toc.ch3": "8 KiB slotted pages & HOT",
  "ch3.law": "PostgreSQL slices every table and index into 8,192-byte slotted pages where line pointers grow downward and tuples grow upward; `UPDATE` appends a fresh version instead of erasing old bytes, and only HOT updates keep indexes out of the loop when both versions share the same page.",
  "ch3.p1": "Suppose you keep an accounting ledger in a paper notebook and realize an earlier amount was wrong. Crossing out the old line cleanly or painting over it hides history from anyone currently reading the ledger; instead, you mark the old entry retired, append the corrected line in the blank space on the same page, and draw an arrow from old to new. PostgreSQL heap pages work exactly this way.",
  "ch3.p2": "Every block is `BLCKSZ = 8,192` bytes (`pg_config_manual.h`). Offset `0` begins with a fixed <strong>24-byte</strong> `PageHeaderData` (`bufpage.h`): 8-byte `pd_lsn`, 2-byte checksum, 2-byte flags, `pd_lower` (end of line pointers), `pd_upper` (start of tuple bytes), `pd_special` (`8192` on heap pages), page layout version, and `pd_prune_xid`. Immediately after byte `24`, <strong>4-byte</strong> `ItemIdData` line pointers grow downward toward higher offsets; row tuples carry a <strong>23-byte</strong> fixed header (aligned to `t_hoff = 24` bytes on 64-bit CPUs, `htup_details.h`) plus 8-byte aligned column data, growing upward from `pd_special`.",
  "dg.pagelayout.cap": "Figure 3. Byte layout of an 8,192-byte heap page: after the 24 B header, 4 B line pointers grow toward higher addresses while tuples grow downward from the bottom, leaving free space in the middle.",
  "dg.pagelayout.aria": "Diagram: 8192-byte slotted page showing 24-byte PageHeaderData, downward ItemIdData slots, free gap, and upward HeapTuple payloads",
  "dg.pagelayout.top": "PageHeaderData (24 B)",
  "dg.pagelayout.hdr": "lsn · flags · lower · upper",
  "dg.pagelayout.items": "ItemIdData (4 B each)",
  "dg.pagelayout.lower": "→ pd_lower",
  "dg.pagelayout.free": "Free space = pd_upper − pd_lower",
  "dg.pagelayout.tuples": "HeapTuple (24 B hdr + data)",
  "dg.pagelayout.hot": "t_ctid chains HOT updates",
  "dg.pagelayout.upper": "← pd_upper",
  "dg.pagelayout.special": "pd_special: Heap=8192 / B-Tree=8176",
  "ch3.p3": "Why does free room inside each page matter so much? When a table keeps headroom (e.g., `fillfactor = 80`), three inserts followed by two updates leave 7,708 free bytes right on the same page. As long as no indexed column changed, PostgreSQL performs a <strong>HOT (Heap-Only Tuple) update</strong>: the old tuple sets `t_ctid` to point to the new slot inside the same page and stamps `HEAP_HOT_UPDATED`, so B-tree indexes do not add a single entry—3 inserts + 2 updates cost just <strong>3</strong> index writes total. Touch an indexed column or overflow the page, however, and even a 1-byte change forces fresh index writes (5 or more).",
  "ch3.model.title": "8,192-byte slotted page simulator: pd_lower, pd_upper & HOT chains",
