  "ch3.say.hot": "<em>{hot} same-page HOT updates</em> succeeded with {free} B free, keeping total index writes at just {idx} because each old row's <code>t_ctid</code> points straight to its successor on this page.",
  "ch3.say.indexed": "Even with {free} B free on the same page, changing an indexed column broke HOT eligibility and forced extra B-tree writes (<em>{idx}</em> index writes total).",
  "ch3.say.full": "Not enough free bytes remained on the page, so the update spilled to a new 8 KiB page, breaking the HOT chain and adding index writes (<em>{idx}</em> writes, {off} off-page).",
  "ch3.limit": "The model leaves out: opportunistic in-page HOT pruning and external TOAST storage when variable-width attributes exceed roughly 2 KiB. Little-endian packing of the 24-byte `PageHeaderData` and `ItemIdData` bitfields is verified byte for byte in `tools/crosscheck.cjs`.",
  "ch4.title": "MVCC & snapshot visibility: why readers never block writers",
  "toc.ch4": "MVCC & snapshot visibility",
  "ch4.law": "Every row version carries the transaction IDs that created it (`xmin`) and retired it (`xmax`); each query takes a lightweight snapshot `{xmin, xmax, xip[]}` and resolves visibility with integer arithmetic—so readers never wait on writers, and writers never wait on readers.",
  "ch4.p1": "If a library locked every shelf whenever a patron opened a book so no clerk could shelve a revised edition, reading would stall editing and editing would freeze reading. PostgreSQL's <strong>Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC)</strong> avoids shared read/write locks altogether: chapter 3 showed how updates leave old tuples behind; this chapter shows how readers pick the right version out of that pile.",
  "ch4.p2": "Every tuple header (`htup_details.h`) records two 32-bit transaction IDs: `t_xmin` (the creator transaction) and `t_xmax` (the deleter or updater, or `0` if alive). When a query starts, `GetSnapshotData()` (`procarray.c`) reads active process entries in shared memory and captures just three numbers: `snap.xmin` (oldest still-running transaction), `snap.xmax` (next unassigned future transaction ID), and `snap.xip[]` (the list of concurrent transactions active inside `[xmin, xmax)` at that instant).",
  "dg.mvtline.cap": "Figure 4. SnapshotData records only three bounds: transactions below xmin committed earlier, transactions at or above xmax had not started, and those in between are checked against xip[].",
  "dg.mvtline.aria": "Diagram: timeline of concurrent transactions T100 through T103 intersected by reader snapshot bounds xmin, xmax, and xip[]",
  "dg.mvtline.sxmin": "snap.xmin=101",
  "dg.mvtline.sxmax": "snap.xmax=104",
  "dg.mvtline.t100": "T100 committed",
  "dg.mvtline.t100s": "xmin < 101 → visible",
  "dg.mvtline.t101": "T101 reader",
  "dg.mvtline.t101s": "Holds snapshot",
  "dg.mvtline.t102": "T102 commits later",
  "dg.mvtline.t102s": "In xip[] → hidden",
  "dg.mvtline.t103": "T103 rolls back",
  "dg.mvtline.t103s": "Aborted → dead",
  "dg.mvtline.rule": "Visible iff xmin committed & xmax not active",
  "ch4.p3": "`HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC` evaluates pure visibility arithmetic: `t_xmin` must be committed and either `t_xmin &lt; snap.xmin` or (`t_xmin &lt; snap.xmax` and not in `snap.xip[]`); meanwhile, if `t_xmax` is nonzero, `t_xmax` must <em>not</em> be visible to the same snapshot. Once a reader checks commit status in `pg_xact`, it sets <strong>Hint Bits</strong> in `t_infomask` (`HEAP_XMIN_COMMITTED`) so subsequent scans skip commit-log lookups completely.",
  "ch4.p4": "Compare isolation modes below: under default <strong>`READ COMMITTED`</strong>, reader T101 captures a fresh snapshot at every statement, so at step 2 it picks up T102's newly committed balance of <strong>`400`</strong>. Switch to <strong>`REPEATABLE READ`</strong>, and T101 freezes the snapshot from statement 1 across the entire transaction—so at steps 2 and 3 it still sees the consistent <strong>`500`</strong>, shielding the read from T102's commit, T103's abort, and T104's in-flight write.",
  "ch4.model.title": "MVCC snapshot & isolation level sandbox",
  "ch4.model.how": "Drag the statement step slider inside reader T101 to watch T102 commit, T103 abort, and T104 start a new write; toggle READ COMMITTED against REPEATABLE READ to see why each tuple version becomes visible or hidden.",
