  "ch4.say.rc": "Under <em>READ COMMITTED</em> at step {step}, T101 took a fresh snapshot [{xmin}, {xmax}) and sees balance <em>{val}</em> (version {ver}). Aborted T103 and in-flight T104 stay cleanly invisible.",
  "ch4.say.rr": "Under <em>REPEATABLE READ</em> at step {step}, T101 reuses step 1's frozen snapshot [{xmin}, {xmax}), so despite T102 committing externally, T101 consistently reads <em>{val}</em> (version {ver}).",
  "ch4.limit": "The model leaves out: intra-transaction `t_cid` visibility for a transaction reading its own earlier writes, and predicate (`SIREAD`) conflict detection under `SERIALIZABLE`. Also note that in `REPEATABLE READ`, attempting to `UPDATE` a row already updated concurrently throws a serialization error rather than silently overwriting.",
  "ch5.title": "VACUUM, FREEZE & 32-bit XID wraparound: why uncleaned tables halt the database",
  "toc.ch5": "VACUUM & 32-bit XID wraparound",
  "ch5.law": "Transaction IDs are 32-bit integers modulo 2³², where half the circle (2³¹ − 1) is treated as the past and the other half as the future; any live row left unfrozen past two billion transactions flips into the \"future\" and vanishes unless `VACUUM FREEZE` marks it permanently visible.",
  "ch5.p1": "Zero read/write blocking and append-only updates carry an obvious debt: retired tuples and aging transaction IDs accumulate relentlessly. `VACUUM` pays two distinct debts without blocking ordinary queries: first, reclaiming dead space and maintaining the <strong>Visibility Map (VM)</strong>; second, <strong>freezing ancient transaction IDs</strong> before the 32-bit clock wraps around.",
  "ch5.p2": "Dead tuples left behind by updates bloat heap pages and slow down every scan. Background `AUTOVACUUM` workers sweep tables online, mark dead tuples and their B-tree entries reusable, and set the `all-visible` bit in the <strong>Visibility Map</strong> whenever every row on an 8 KiB page is visible to all active transactions—that single VM bit is what unlocks chapter 2's heap-skipping <strong>Index-Only Scan</strong>.",
  "ch5.p3": "The deeper deadline is <strong>32-bit XID wraparound</strong>. Because `TransactionId` is 32 bits wide (~4.29 billion IDs) and `varsup.c` / `transam.c` compare ages with signed modular subtraction `((int32)(id1 - id2)) &lt; 0`, any point up to <strong>2,147.5 million</strong> (`2³¹ − 1`) steps behind `CurrentXID` counts as past, while the opposite half of the dial counts as future. Left unfrozen, a live row inserted at XID 1,000,000 suddenly crosses the `2³¹` boundary two billion transactions later and becomes invisible to every snapshot! That is why PostgreSQL triggers emergency anti-wraparound vacuum at <strong>`autovacuum_freeze_max_age` = 200M</strong> to stamp ancient rows `HEAP_XMIN_FROZEN`, and enters `xidStopLimit` around <strong>2,144.5M</strong> to refuse new writes before data disappears.",
  "dg.xidcircle.cap": "Figure 5. The 32-bit XID clock (modulo 2³²): the trailing semicircle (2³¹ − 1) is past; any unfrozen live row pushed past the bottom line wraps into the red future half.",
  "dg.xidcircle.aria": "Diagram: 32-bit circular XID clock showing CurrentXID at top, green past semicircle, red future semicircle, 200M freeze trigger, and 2^31 wrap boundary",
